data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The French","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Revolution (1789–1799) stands as one of the most transformative","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" events in modern history. It dismantled the centuries-old structure of absolute monarchy and feudal privilege, replacing them with the radical","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" ideals of \"Liberté, égalité, fraternité.\" The causes of the revolution were a volatile mixture of economic distress","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", social inequality, and intellectual ferment, while its consequences reshaped the political landscape of Europe and the world.\n\n### The","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Causes: A Perfect Storm\n\nThe origins of the French Revolution were multifaceted, rooted in the structural failures of the *Ancien Régime*.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\n\n**1. Social Inequality and the Estate System**\nFrench society was divided into three estates. The First Estate (cler","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"gy) and the Second Estate (nobility) owned the vast majority of the land, held the most prestigious government positions, and were","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" largely exempt from taxation. The Third Estate, comprising 98% of the population—ranging from wealthy merchants and lawyers to impoverished","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" peasants—bore the entire tax burden. This systemic inequality created deep-seated resentment, as the Third Estate felt excluded from political","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" power despite being the backbone of the nation’s economy.\n\n**2. Economic Crisis**\nBy the late 1","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"780s, France was on the brink of bankruptcy. Decades of extravagant royal spending, coupled with the massive costs","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of financing the American Revolution, had drained the treasury. Simultaneously, a series of poor harvests led to severe food shortages and skyrocketing","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" bread prices. As the peasantry faced starvation and the urban working class (the *sans-culottes*) struggled to survive, the monarchy","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"’s inability to address these crises eroded its legitimacy.\n\n**3. The Enlightenment**\nThe intellectual climate of the ","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"18th century provided the ideological framework for the revolution. Philosophers like Rousseau, Voltaire, and Montesquieu challenged","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the \"divine right of kings\" and advocated for concepts such as popular sovereignty, separation of powers, and individual rights. These ideas circulated","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" among the educated bourgeoisie, who began to envision a government based on reason and representation rather than hereditary privilege.\n\n**4.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Weak Leadership**\nKing Louis XVI was an indecisive and often detached ruler. His failure to implement meaningful fiscal reforms—larg","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

