data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of Napoleon Bonaparte. Its consequences were profound and enduring.\n\n**1. The End of Feudalism and Absolute Monarchy**\nThe","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" most immediate consequence was the total destruction of the feudal system. The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" adopted in 1789, codified the principles of equality before the law, freedom of speech, and freedom of religion. The","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" revolution effectively ended the absolute power of the monarch and the special privileges of the aristocracy, establishing the precedent that sovereignty resides in the people, not the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" crown.\n\n**2. The Rise of Nationalism**\nThe revolution transformed the French people from \"subjects\" of a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" king into \"citizens\" of a nation. This shift fostered a powerful sense of national identity. The mobilization of the French populace","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

